================================================================================
                    GROUP BY CLAUSE IN SQL - COMPLETE GUIDE
================================================================================

WHAT IS GROUP BY?
-----------------
The GROUP BY clause is used to group rows that have the same values into summary rows.
It is mainly used with aggregate functions like:
  • COUNT()
  • SUM()
  • AVG()
  • MAX()
  • MIN()

================================================================================
SYNTAX OF GROUP BY
================================================================================

  SELECT column_name, aggregate_function(column_name)
  FROM table_name
  GROUP BY column_name;

================================================================================
EXAMPLE TABLE: EMPLOYEES
================================================================================

+--------+------------+--------+
| emp_id | department | salary |
+--------+------------+--------+
| 1      | HR         | 30000  |
| 2      | IT         | 50000  |
| 3      | HR         | 35000  |
| 4      | IT         | 60000  |
| 5      | Sales      | 40000  |
+--------+------------+--------+

================================================================================
EXAMPLE 1 – COUNT EMPLOYEES DEPARTMENT WISE
================================================================================

Query:
  SELECT department, COUNT(*) AS total_employees
  FROM Employees
  GROUP BY department;

Output:
+------------+------------------+
| department | total_employees  |
+------------+------------------+
| HR         | 2                |
| IT         | 2                |
| Sales      | 1                |
+------------+------------------+

GROUP BY – Order of Execution (Step-by-Step):
  Step 1: Take all rows from table.
  Step 2: Group rows based on same department.
          HR    → 2 rows
          IT    → 2 rows
          Sales → 1 row
  Step 3: Apply aggregate function COUNT(*).
  Step 4: Return grouped result.

================================================================================
EXAMPLE 2 – SUM OF SALARIES
================================================================================

Query:
  SELECT department, SUM(salary) AS total_salary
  FROM Employees
  GROUP BY department;

Output:
+------------+--------------+
| department | total_salary |
+------------+--------------+
| HR         | 65000        |
| IT         | 110000       |
| Sales      | 40000        |
+------------+--------------+

================================================================================
IMPORTANT RULES OF GROUP BY
================================================================================

Rule 1: Columns in SELECT must be:
        • Present in GROUP BY
          OR
        • Used with aggregate functions

Correct Example:
  SELECT department, COUNT(*)
  FROM Employees
  GROUP BY department;

Wrong Example:
  SELECT emp_id, department, COUNT(*)
  FROM Employees
  GROUP BY department;

Why wrong? Because emp_id is neither:
  • grouped
  • nor aggregated

================================================================================
AGGREGATE FUNCTIONS USED WITH GROUP BY
================================================================================

+----------+----------------+
| Function | Purpose        |
+----------+----------------+
| COUNT()  | Count rows     |
| SUM()    | Total          |
| AVG()    | Average        |
| MAX()    | Highest value  |
