================================================================================
HAVING CLAUSE IN SQL
================================================================================

What is HAVING?
  The HAVING clause is used to filter grouped data.
  It works after GROUP BY.

Difference Between WHERE and HAVING:

+----------------------------------+----------------------------------+
| WHERE                            | HAVING                           |
+----------------------------------+----------------------------------+
| Filters rows                     | Filters groups                   |
| Used before grouping             | Used after grouping              |
| Cannot use aggregate functions   | Can use aggregate functions      |
| directly                         |                                  |
+----------------------------------+----------------------------------+

================================================================================
SYNTAX OF HAVING
================================================================================

  SELECT column_name, aggregate_function(column_name)
  FROM table_name
  GROUP BY column_name
  HAVING condition;

================================================================================
EXAMPLE – DEPARTMENTS WITH TOTAL SALARY > 70000
================================================================================

Query:
  SELECT department, SUM(salary) AS total_salary
  FROM Employees
  GROUP BY department
  HAVING SUM(salary) > 70000;

Output:
+------------+--------------+
| department | total_salary |
+------------+--------------+
| IT         | 110000       |
+------------+--------------+

HAVING – Order of Execution (Step-by-Step):
  Step 1: Take all rows.
  Step 2: Group rows by department.
  Step 3: Calculate SUM(salary):
          HR    → 65000
          IT    → 110000
          Sales → 40000
  Step 4: Apply HAVING condition: SUM(salary) > 70000
  Step 5: Return matching groups only: IT → 110000

================================================================================
WHERE vs HAVING EXAMPLES
================================================================================

WHERE Example (filters rows before grouping):
  SELECT department, SUM(salary)
  FROM Employees
  WHERE salary > 35000
  GROUP BY department;

HAVING Example (filters groups after grouping):
  SELECT department, SUM(salary)
  FROM Employees
  GROUP BY department
  HAVING SUM(salary) > 70000;

================================================================================
SQL QUERY EXECUTION ORDER (Actual)
================================================================================

Order  Clause
--------------------------------
1      FROM
2      WHERE
3      GROUP BY
4      HAVING
5      SELECT
6      ORDER BY
7      LIMIT

================================================================================
COMPLETE EXAMPLE WITH EXECUTION FLOW
================================================================================

Query:
  SELECT department, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary
  FROM Employees
  WHERE salary > 30000
  GROUP BY department
  HAVING AVG(salary) > 45000
  ORDER BY avg_salary DESC;

Execution Flow:
  Step 1 (FROM)      → Take Employees table
  Step 2 (WHERE)     → Filter salary > 30000
  Step 3 (GROUP BY)  → Group rows by department
  Step 4 (HAVING)    → Keep groups with AVG(salary) > 45000
  Step 5 (SELECT)    → Choose columns
  Step 6 (ORDER BY)  → Sort descending

================================================================================
QUICK REVISION
================================================================================

GROUP BY  → Used to group rows
HAVING    → Used to filter groups

Simple Memory Trick:
  WHERE    → Filter rows
  GROUP BY → Make groups
  HAVING   → Filter groups

================================================================================
INTERVIEW POINTS
================================================================================

1. GROUP BY groups similar rows.
2. HAVING filters grouped data.
3. WHERE works before GROUP BY.
4. HAVING works after GROUP BY.
5. Aggregate functions are commonly used with GROUP BY.
6. HAVING can use aggregate functions.
7. WHERE cannot directly filter aggregated results.
